From af498e28b99e128eab5f34d509d0d30bdf426eb2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gavin John <gavinnjohn@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 20:12:21 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 38/41] aout: correct drift with a PI controller instead of a
 relay

The drift correction was a relay: outside the EBU tolerance it picked a
direction, stepped the resampling rate by a fixed permille per buffer
until the drift had halved, then reversed. Two things follow from that.
It never settles - the only way it can stop is by driving the rate back
through zero, so it hunts around the set point instead of sitting on it.
And it cannot hold a standing rate offset, which is precisely what a
device whose clock does not run at exactly the nominal sample rate needs
to be kept in sync with: every time the correction is unwound the drift
starts building again from the same cause.

Feed the drift to a PI controller instead, whose output is the offset
given to the resampler rather than an increment of it. The proportional
gain is set so that term alone asks for the whole of the resampling bound
at one AOUT_MAX_PTS_DELAY of drift: anything outside the tolerance is
still corrected as fast as the bound allows. The integral term converges
on the device's own rate offset and holds it, so the steady state is zero
drift at a non-zero rate offset - a state the relay had no way to express.
There is no derivative term: the drift is quantised by whatever
granularity the output reports its delay with, and differentiating that
gives noise, not a trend.

Windup is guarded by integrating conditionally - never while the output
is pinned at the bound and the drift pushes it further in - and by
clamping the integral to the bound in its own right. The interval used
is measured rather than assumed, and capped, so a stall in the decoder
is not taken as evidence of drift for its whole length.

The controller is held, rather than reset, whenever the drift reading is
known to be stale: while a jump is outstanding or still working through
what the output already holds, and across a discontinuity. The
accumulated correction survives a flush, a pause and an output restart,
which is the point of it - it describes the device, not the stream.

Simulated against a device 100 ppm fast with a 60 ms initial drift and
its reported delay quantised to 10 ms with 2 ms of noise, the drift is
back within 5 ms after 20 s and stays within 3.5 ms thereafter, with the
integral settled on 0.0998 permille against the 0.1 permille the device
actually wants. With the device 10 permille off - past the bound - the
correction pins at the bound and the integral stops growing; the jump
path takes over as before.

aout_FiltersAdjustResampling() is replaced by a setter taking an absolute
offset as a fraction of the sample rate, plus an accessor for the bound,
both of them core-internal like aout_FiltersCanResample() was. The bound
no longer has to be even now that nothing steps towards it in twos.

The relay's "timing screwed" escape goes with it: it measured the drift
against the value it had at the instant the relay engaged, which a
controller with no engagement has no equivalent of. Gross lateness is
already handled ahead of the correction by the one-at-a-time jump.

Gains are exposed as "aout-drift-gain" and "aout-drift-integral-gain" so
libvlc callers can retune them; the defaults need no configuration.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
